// Economics Capstone — exams.js (rewritten: all original questions)
module.exports = {
  "midterm": {
    "title": "Economics Core Comprehensive I",
    "minutes": 90,
    "passPercent": 70,
    "coverage": "Microeconomics, macroeconomics & statistics core. Practice exam — original questions in the app's own course style. 70% is the app's own course standard, not a credential claim.",
    "questions": [
      {"module": 1, "q": "A student proposes to study whether a $15 minimum wage changed teen employment in Michigan counties. A classmate proposes the topic 'minimum wages.' Which one is a research question, and why?", "choices": ["The $15 proposal — it names a policy, a place, a population, and an outcome to measure", "The topic 'minimum wages' — it is broader, so it is a better question", "Neither — economics questions must be about the whole country", "Both are equally good research questions"], "answer": 0, "explanation": "Correct: (a). A research question names what is being tested, on whom, and where — the $15 proposal does all three. (b) is wrong: broader is worse, not better; a topic with no question in it cannot be tested. (c) is wrong: a county-level question is a perfectly good research question. (d) is wrong: a topic is not a question."},
      {"module": 1, "q": "A classmate re-runs a published paper's regression on the same data, gets the same answer, and calls it a capstone. What is missing?", "choices": ["An original contribution — a new question, new data, or a new angle on the old question", "A longer literature review", "More decimal places", "A bigger font on the title page"], "answer": 0, "explanation": "Correct: (a). A capstone must add something of the student's own; reproducing someone else's result on their data adds nothing. (b) is wrong: a longer review does not make the work original. (c) is wrong: precision is not originality. (d) is wrong: formatting is not a contribution."},
      {"module": 1, "q": "Your abstract says the slope is 1.8; your results table says 2.1. A reader notices. What does this tell you?", "choices": ["Nothing — small differences do not matter", "The end-to-end read failed: one number must be wrong, and every place it appears must be checked", "The abstract is always right", "The table is always right"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "Correct: (b). One number cannot be both 1.8 and 2.1; the inconsistency means an error slipped through, and the read must catch it everywhere the number appears. (a) is wrong: a reader who finds one wrong number stops trusting the rest. (c) is wrong: the abstract is written last and can carry a typo. (d) is wrong: tables can carry typos too."},
      {"module": 1, "q": "A job posting asks whether finishing this capstone makes you a licensed economist. What is the honest answer?", "choices": ["Yes — the capstone is a license", "No — there is no license to practice economics; the paper is the evidence of what you can do", "Yes, once Bloomberg Market Concepts is complete", "No — only a CFA charter licenses an economist"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "Correct: (b). Economics has no license and no entry-level scored credential; the finished paper is the takeaway. (a) is wrong: no such license exists. (c) is wrong: Bloomberg Market Concepts is a certificate of completion, not a license. (d) is wrong: the CFA is a finance credential, and it does not license anyone as an economist."},
      {"module": 1, "q": "Bloomberg Market Concepts is tracked as completed, not scored. How should you describe it on a résumé?", "choices": ["As a certificate of completion, with no score claimed", "As a licensing exam you passed", "As a degree in finance", "As proof you scored in the top 10%"], "answer": 0, "explanation": "Correct: (a). Completion is what it is — claiming a score or a pass mark invents a credential that does not exist. (b) is wrong: it is not a licensing exam. (c) is wrong: it is a short certificate, not a degree. (d) is wrong: there is no score to rank."},
      {"module": 2, "q": "A student asks: 'Does living near a bus line raise attendance at a community college?' What makes this a research question rather than a topic?", "choices": ["It names the population, the two variables, and the relationship to test", "It is about buses", "It can be answered yes or no with no data", "It is short"], "answer": 0, "explanation": "Correct: (a). Population (community college students), variables (distance to a bus line, attendance), relationship (raises) — all three are there. (b) is wrong: the subject alone does not make a question. (c) is wrong: a question that needs no data is not a research question. (d) is wrong: length is not the test."},
      {"module": 2, "q": "Which of these claims is falsifiable?", "choices": ["'Studying more raises grades' — a study could find no difference between studiers and non-studiers", "'Studying is good for the soul'", "'Education is the most important thing in life'", "'Students should study more'"], "answer": 0, "explanation": "Correct: (a). A result exists that would count against it — no grade difference — so it can be tested. (b) is wrong: 'good for the soul' cannot be measured or disproved. (c) is wrong: 'most important' is a value judgment. (d) is wrong: 'should' is a prescription, not a testable claim."},
      {"module": 2, "q": "A student regresses monthly rent on distance from campus in miles. The null hypothesis is:", "choices": ["Distance raises rent", "The slope on distance is zero", "The slope is negative", "Rent cannot be measured"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "Correct: (b). H0 claims no effect: the slope equals zero. (a) is wrong: that is the alternative hypothesis. (c) is wrong: a negative slope is a specific alternative, not the null. (d) is wrong: rent is measured in dollars."},
      {"module": 2, "q": "A study compares scholarship recipients with similar students who did not get one. Which narrowing cut does the comparison group answer?", "choices": ["Who", "What", "How much", "Compared to what"], "answer": 3, "explanation": "Correct: (d). 'Compared to what' names the comparison group — similar students without the scholarship. (a) is wrong: 'who' names the population. (b) is wrong: 'what' names the variables. (c) is wrong: 'how much' names the effect size."},
      {"module": 2, "q": "A regression of test scores on class size omits family income. Wealthier districts have smaller classes, and income raises scores. The class-size slope is biased:", "choices": ["Upward — it looks more positive than it is", "Downward — the omitted income effect pushes it negative, so small classes look better than they are", "Not at all — income is not in the data", "Upward, because small classes cause higher income"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "Correct: (b). Bias equals the omitted effect (income raises scores: positive) times the link between the omitted and included variables (income is negatively related to class size), which is negative — so the slope is pushed down and small classes get too much credit. (a) is wrong: the bias is negative, not positive. (c) is wrong: leaving a variable out is exactly what creates the bias. (d) is wrong: the causation runs from income to class size here, not the other way."},
      {"module": 3, "q": "You have 12 papers on class size: 5 find smaller classes help, 4 find no effect, 3 find they hurt. How should the literature review be organized?", "choices": ["One paragraph per paper, in the order you found them", "By theme — what the studies agree on, where they disagree, and what is missing", "By the authors' last names", "By publication date, oldest first"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "Correct: (b). Synthesis groups by theme so the reader learns what is known and where the disagreement sits. (a) is wrong: that is a list, not a synthesis. (c) is wrong: alphabetical order is a bibliography, not a review. (d) is wrong: date order is not an argument."},
      {"module": 3, "q": "Surveys put the typical return to a year of schooling at roughly 5–10%. A new study claims 40% per year. What should a reviewer suspect first?", "choices": ["That the study is a breakthrough to celebrate", "A measurement problem, a selected sample, or an omitted variable inflating the estimate", "That Card (1999) was wrong about everything", "That 40% is within the normal range"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "Correct: (b). A result four times the surveyed range is a signal to check the study, not to rewrite the literature. (a) is wrong: extraordinary claims need extraordinary checking first. (c) is wrong: one outlier does not overturn a whole survey. (d) is wrong: 40% is far outside 5–10%."},
      {"module": 3, "q": "A study of one city's workers finds each extra year of schooling associated with 12% higher pay — above the literature's 5–10% range. Which explanation fits ability bias?", "choices": ["The city's able workers get more schooling and earn more anyway, so the slope absorbs the ability effect", "The city has no able workers", "The literature range is a law of nature", "12% is below the range"], "answer": 0, "explanation": "Correct: (a). Ability is omitted, it raises both schooling and wages, so the slope is biased upward — exactly what an above-range estimate looks like. (b) is wrong: ability exists everywhere. (c) is wrong: the range is an empirical summary, not a law. (d) is wrong: 12% is above 10%."},
      {"module": 3, "q": "Why does quarter of birth work as an instrument for schooling in Angrist and Krueger (1991), rather than just being another correlate of wages?", "choices": ["Because birthdays determine ability", "Because compulsory-schooling laws made some children stay in school longer for reasons unrelated to ability, so it shifts schooling without directly affecting wages", "Because winter babies earn more", "Because it was the only variable they could measure"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "Correct: (b). The law-plus-birthday interaction assigned extra schooling by the calendar, not by ability — that is what makes it an instrument instead of a mere correlate. (a) is wrong: birthdays do not determine ability. (c) is wrong: that is not the finding. (d) is wrong: the design was chosen for identification, not convenience."},
      {"module": 3, "q": "A literature review cites only the three studies that agree with the author's hypothesis and ignores seven that disagree. What is this called, and why is it a problem?", "choices": ["Cherry-picking — the reader cannot trust the review's verdict on what is known", "Good focus — disagreeing studies are irrelevant", "Standard practice", "A meta-analysis"], "answer": 0, "explanation": "Correct: (a). A review that hides disagreement misleads the reader about the state of knowledge. (b) is wrong: disagreeing studies are the most important ones to report. (c) is wrong: selective citation is a breach of honesty, not standard practice. (d) is wrong: a meta-analysis pools all the studies, it does not hide them."},
      {"module": 4, "q": "A dataset follows 200 graduates' earnings every year for 5 years after graduation. What kind of data is this?", "choices": ["Cross-sectional", "Time-series", "Panel — many workers followed over time", "Experimental"], "answer": 2, "explanation": "Correct: (c). Many units observed over many periods is panel data. (a) is wrong: cross-sectional data observes many units once. (b) is wrong: time series follows one unit over time. (d) is wrong: nothing was randomly assigned here."},
      {"module": 4, "q": "A regression finds rent = 400 + 25 × rooms, where rent is monthly dollars and rooms is the number of bedrooms. The slope of 25 means:", "choices": ["Rent is $400 with no bedrooms", "Each extra bedroom is associated with $25 more rent per month", "The average rent is $25", "The error for one apartment is $25"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "Correct: (b). The slope is the predicted change in rent for one more bedroom — $25 a month. (a) is wrong: $400 is the intercept, the rent at zero bedrooms. (c) is wrong: $25 is the slope, not the average rent. (d) is wrong: the error is the gap between an actual rent and its prediction."},
      {"module": 4, "q": "A regression line passes through the point of means (mean x = 8, mean y = 50) and has a slope of 3. What is the intercept?", "choices": ["50", "24", "26", "8"], "answer": 2, "explanation": "Correct: (c). The intercept is mean y minus slope times mean x: 50 − 3 × 8 = 26. (a) is wrong: 50 is the mean of y, not the intercept. (b) is wrong: 24 is the slope times mean x, only half the calculation. (d) is wrong: 8 is the mean of x."},
      {"module": 4, "q": "A regression finds score = 20 + 6 × hours, where score is a test score and hours is study time. The intercept of 20 means:", "choices": ["The predicted score for a student who studies zero hours", "The average test score", "The slope of the line", "A student who studies 20 hours scores 6"], "answer": 0, "explanation": "Correct: (a). The intercept is the prediction when hours equal zero — 20 points. (b) is wrong: the average score depends on the average hours studied. (c) is wrong: the slope is 6. (d) is wrong: it mixes the two numbers up — 20 hours predicts 20 + 6 × 20 = 140."},
      {"module": 4, "q": "Why does OLS square the residuals instead of simply adding them up?", "choices": ["Squaring makes the arithmetic easier", "Plain residuals sum to zero by construction, so their sum cannot measure fit — squaring stops positives and negatives from cancelling", "Squaring removes the intercept", "OLS never uses residuals"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "Correct: (b). With an intercept, OLS residuals always sum to zero, so adding them tells you nothing; squaring makes every gap count. (a) is wrong: squaring is harder arithmetic, done for a reason. (c) is wrong: squaring has nothing to do with the intercept. (d) is wrong: OLS is defined by the residuals — it minimizes their squared sum."},
      {"module": 5, "q": "Nine households in a town earn $40,000 each; one household earns $400,000. Which number better describes the typical household, and why?", "choices": ["The mean, $76,000 — it uses all the data", "The median, $40,000 — the one very high income drags the mean far above what anyone typical earns", "The mean, $40,000", "Neither — the typical household earns $400,000"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "Correct: (b). The mean is (9 × 40,000 + 400,000) ÷ 10 = $76,000, nearly double what nine of the ten households earn; the median of $40,000 is the typical household. (a) is wrong: using all the data is not the point when one value distorts the average. (c) is wrong: $40,000 is the median, not the mean. (d) is wrong: one household out of ten is not typical."},
      {"module": 5, "q": "Two classes have the same mean test score of 75. Class A has a standard deviation of 3; Class B has a standard deviation of 12. What differs between the classes?", "choices": ["Their average performance", "Their spread — Class B's scores are far more scattered around the mean", "Their size", "Their median, which must be 75 in both"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "Correct: (b). The standard deviation measures spread around the mean; 12 means much wider scattering than 3. (a) is wrong: the means are the same, 75. (c) is wrong: nothing here says anything about class size. (d) is wrong: the median need not equal the mean in either class."},
      {"module": 5, "q": "A sample of 4 values has a mean of 10, and the squared deviations from the mean sum to 36. What is the sample variance?", "choices": ["9", "36", "12", "6"], "answer": 2, "explanation": "Correct: (c). The sample variance divides by n − 1: 36 ÷ 3 = 12. (a) is wrong: 9 comes from dividing by n = 4, which understates the spread. (b) is wrong: 36 is the sum of squared deviations, not the variance. (d) is wrong: 6 is the square root of 36, not the variance."},
      {"module": 5, "q": "Workers with a two-year certificate earn $28 an hour on average; similar workers without it earn $20. What payoff per year of the certificate does that imply?", "choices": ["$28 per year", "$8 per year", "$4 per year", "$20 per year"], "answer": 2, "explanation": "Correct: (c). The $8 gap is spread over the 2 years the certificate takes: (28 − 20) ÷ 2 = $4 per year. (a) is wrong: $28 is the certificate holders' wage, not the payoff. (b) is wrong: $8 is the total gap, not the per-year figure. (d) is wrong: $20 is the non-holders' wage."},
      {"module": 5, "q": "Which sentence belongs in a descriptive results section about a gym study?", "choices": ["'Going to the gym causes weight loss'", "'Members who attend three times a week weigh 8 pounds less on average than members who attend once a week'", "'The government should subsidize gyms'", "'The regression proves the hypothesis'"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "Correct: (b). It reports what the data shows, with numbers, and claims nothing about causation. (a) is wrong: causation is an interpretation claim. (c) is wrong: policy belongs in the implications section. (d) is wrong: description reports; the regression section tests."},
      {"module": 6, "q": "A regression of crop yield on fertilizer finds a slope of 1.8 with a standard error of 0.45. The t-statistic is 4.0 and the 5% critical value is 2.0. What do you conclude?", "choices": ["Fail to reject the null of no effect", "Reject the null — the slope is statistically significant", "The slope is causal", "The sample proves fertilizer always works"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "Correct: (b). 4.0 > 2.0, so the null of a zero slope is rejected. (a) is wrong: that is what a small t would give. (c) is wrong: significance is not causation. (d) is wrong: one regression on one crop proves nothing about always."},
      {"module": 6, "q": "For the same fertilizer regression (slope 1.8, standard error 0.45, critical value 2.0), the 95% confidence interval is:", "choices": ["0.90 to 2.70", "1.35 to 2.25", "0.00 to 1.80", "−0.45 to 1.80"], "answer": 0, "explanation": "Correct: (a). 1.8 ± 2.0 × 0.45 = 1.8 ± 0.90 = 0.90 to 2.70. (b) is wrong: that uses a margin of 0.45, one standard error instead of two. (c) is wrong: the interval does not include zero. (d) is wrong: those are the negative standard error and the slope, not an interval."},
      {"module": 6, "q": "In a simple regression, the correlation between the two variables is 0.9. What is R²?", "choices": ["0.90", "0.81", "0.45", "1.80"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "Correct: (b). In a simple regression R² is the squared correlation: 0.9 × 0.9 = 0.81. (a) is wrong: that is the correlation itself. (c) is wrong: 0.45 is half the correlation, which has no role here. (d) is wrong: that is twice the correlation."},
      {"module": 6, "q": "The fertilizer regression is yield = 20 + 1.8 × fertilizer, where yield is bushels per acre and fertilizer is kilograms per acre. The predicted yield at 10 kilograms is:", "choices": ["20 bushels", "38 bushels", "18 bushels", "200 bushels"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "Correct: (b). 20 + 1.8 × 10 = 38 bushels. (a) is wrong: that is the intercept, the prediction at zero fertilizer. (c) is wrong: that is 1.8 × 10 alone, without the intercept. (d) is wrong: that multiplies the intercept by the fertilizer instead of adding."},
      {"module": 6, "q": "The fertilizer study used 0 to 20 kilograms per acre. A farmer asks what yield to expect at 40 kilograms. What is the right answer?", "choices": ["38 bushels — just extend the line", "You cannot say from this study: 40 is outside the observed range, and the relationship may flatten or turn down", "Zero — fertilizer stops working at 20", "Double the yield at 20 kilograms, exactly"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "Correct: (b). Extrapolation assumes the straight line holds where there is no data, and it often does not. (a) is wrong: 38 is the prediction at 10 kilograms, and extending the line is the mistake. (c) is wrong: no data does not mean zero yield. (d) is wrong: doubling is a guess, not a result."},
      {"module": 7, "q": "A study of 200,000 workers finds each extra year of schooling raises wages by $0.15 an hour, with t = 6.2. What is the right verdict?", "choices": ["A huge effect — the t-statistic is large", "Statistically significant but economically trivial: $0.15 an hour is about $300 a year", "Not significant — the sample is too big", "Proof that schooling causes higher wages"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "Correct: (b). $0.15 × 2,000 hours = $300 a year — real, but tiny; the huge sample is what makes it significant. (a) is wrong: the t-statistic measures precision, not size. (c) is wrong: a big sample makes significance easier, not harder. (d) is wrong: significance is not causation."},
      {"module": 7, "q": "A study finds towns with more libraries have higher test scores. Which rival story is most plausible?", "choices": ["Libraries cause test scores directly, with no other explanation possible", "Wealthier towns can afford both more libraries and better schools, so wealth drives both", "Test scores cause libraries to be built", "The data was typed correctly"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "Correct: (b). Wealth is an omitted variable that plausibly raises both libraries and scores. (a) is wrong: it rules out every alternative by assertion. (c) is wrong: scores do not build libraries. (d) is wrong: typing is a data-quality check, not a rival explanation."},
      {"module": 7, "q": "Cities with more police officers tend to have more crime. Reverse causation here would mean:", "choices": ["Police cause crime", "High crime leads cities to hire more police — the arrow runs from crime to police, not police to crime", "The regression was run backwards", "Police have no effect on crime"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "Correct: (b). The arrow runs from expected crime to police hiring, not from police to crime. (a) is wrong: that is the opposite causal claim. (c) is wrong: the regression direction is a modeling choice, not causation. (d) is wrong: reverse causation says nothing about whether police reduce crime."},
      {"module": 7, "q": "A researcher finds a correlation of 0.7 between cups of coffee drunk and lines of code written in one office. Which sentence may she write?", "choices": ["'Coffee causes programmers to write more code'", "'In this office, coffee drinking is strongly associated with more lines of code'", "'Every programmer writes 70% more code after coffee'", "'Coffee raises productivity in all offices'"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "Correct: (b). It claims association in this sample, with the number behind it. (a) is wrong: causation is not established. (c) is wrong: 0.7 is a correlation, not a 70% gain, and it is an average, not a promise to each programmer. (d) is wrong: one office does not generalize to all offices."},
      {"module": 7, "q": "A 95% confidence interval for the payoff to an hour of training is $0.05 to $0.09, and the mean wage is $25 an hour. What does this show?", "choices": ["A large effect — the interval excludes zero", "Statistical significance with economic triviality: even the top end is only 0.36% of the mean wage", "No effect — the interval is narrow", "Proof the training causes higher wages"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "Correct: (b). $0.09 ÷ $25 = 0.36% — the whole interval is tiny next to the wage, even though it excludes zero. (a) is wrong: excluding zero is significance, not size. (c) is wrong: narrowness is precision, and the interval excludes zero. (d) is wrong: no interval proves causation."},
      {"module": 8, "q": "An extra year of schooling pays $3,000 a year for 30 years. At a 5% discount rate its present value is about $46,100. What does that number mean?", "choices": ["The total undiscounted pay over 30 years", "What the whole 30-year stream of benefits is worth in today's dollars", "The cost of the year of schooling", "The wage after 30 years"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "Correct: (b). Present value translates future dollars into today's dollars — $46,100 is the 30-year stream priced today. (a) is wrong: the undiscounted total is $3,000 × 30 = $90,000. (c) is wrong: the cost is a separate number, compared against this benefit. (d) is wrong: the benefit is a yearly flow, not a wage at year 30."},
      {"module": 8, "q": "A training program charges $2,000 tuition and takes 4 weeks, during which a worker gives up pay of $800 a week. What is the full cost of the program?", "choices": ["$2,000", "$3,200", "$5,200", "$800"], "answer": 2, "explanation": "Correct: (c). $2,000 tuition + 4 × $800 = $2,000 + $3,200 = $5,200. (a) is wrong: tuition is only part of the cost — the forgone pay counts too. (b) is wrong: $3,200 is the forgone pay alone. (d) is wrong: $800 is one week's pay."},
      {"module": 8, "q": "The present value of a $3,000-a-year benefit for 30 years is about $46,100 at a 5% discount rate but only about $33,800 at 8%. What does the drop show?", "choices": ["Higher discount rates make future benefits worth less today, so the policy case weakens as the rate rises", "The benefit itself shrinks when rates rise", "Discount rates do not matter", "The benefit becomes negative at 8%"], "answer": 0, "explanation": "Correct: (a). Discounting harder shrinks today's value of the same future dollars — the benefit stream is unchanged, its present value is not. (b) is wrong: the $3,000 a year is the same at either rate. (c) is wrong: the value fell by more than a quarter when the rate rose. (d) is wrong: $33,800 is still positive."},
      {"module": 8, "q": "Two workers consider the same $2,000 course that takes 100 hours. One earns $15 an hour, the other $40. Whose total cost is lower, and by how much?", "choices": ["The $15 worker — $3,500 total versus $6,000, a $2,500 difference", "The $40 worker — higher pay means lower cost", "Both pay the same $2,000", "The $15 worker by $500"], "answer": 0, "explanation": "Correct: (a). The $15 worker gives up $1,500 in pay (total $3,500); the $40 worker gives up $4,000 (total $6,000) — a $2,500 gap. (b) is wrong: higher pay means higher forgone wages, not lower cost. (c) is wrong: tuition is the same, but forgone pay differs. (d) is wrong: the gap is $2,500, not $500."},
      {"module": 8, "q": "A small pilot doubles the number of nursing graduates in one town and their wages hold steady. A national program doubles nursing graduates everywhere. What might the pilot miss?", "choices": ["Nothing — pilots always scale exactly", "A general-equilibrium effect: flooding the whole market could push nurses' wages down", "The t-statistic", "The cost of the pilot"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "Correct: (b). One town's pilot cannot show what happens when the whole market's supply changes. (a) is wrong: scaling changes the market the pilot held fixed. (c) is wrong: the t-statistic is a property of the pilot's regression. (d) is wrong: cost is a separate question from the market effect."},
      {"module": 9, "q": "Your abstract says the slope is statistically significant, but the results table shows a p-value of 0.12. What must you do before submitting?", "choices": ["Nothing — abstracts are not checked", "Fix the inconsistency: either the abstract is wrong or the table is, and the number must match everywhere", "Delete the table", "Delete the abstract"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "Correct: (b). A p-value of 0.12 is not significant at 5%, so the abstract and the table contradict each other — one of them is wrong. (a) is wrong: readers check the abstract against the tables first. (c) is wrong: deleting evidence hides the problem. (d) is wrong: every paper needs an abstract."},
      {"module": 9, "q": "A reader asks what the stars next to the slope in Table 2 mean. Where should the answer already be?", "choices": ["In the table's notes line, which defines the stars and the significance levels", "In the abstract", "Nowhere — readers are expected to guess", "In the appendix only"], "answer": 0, "explanation": "Correct: (a). Table notes say what the numbers are — what the stars mean, the dependent variable, the method. (b) is wrong: the abstract summarizes results, it does not define symbols. (c) is wrong: unexplained symbols are a drafting failure. (d) is wrong: the notes belong with the table, where the reader is looking."},
      {"module": 9, "q": "Your draft cites a paper in the text, but it is missing from the references list. What happens?", "choices": ["Nothing — references are decorative", "The reader cannot find the source, so the citation fails; add it to the references", "The citation becomes stronger", "The paper is automatically rejected for length"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "Correct: (b). A citation is a pointer; a pointer to nowhere is broken, and the fix is to add the reference. (a) is wrong: references are how readers check your claims. (c) is wrong: a missing reference weakens the citation. (d) is wrong: length has nothing to do with it."},
      {"module": 9, "q": "Which belongs in the appendix rather than the main text?", "choices": ["The headline result", "The full 10-row dataset and the step-by-step regression arithmetic", "The abstract", "The conclusion"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "Correct: (b). The appendix holds the material that lets a reader check the work without interrupting the argument. (a) is wrong: the headline result belongs in the results section. (c) is wrong: the abstract opens the paper. (d) is wrong: the conclusion closes it."},
      {"module": 9, "q": "Your conclusion introduces a brand-new policy proposal that appears nowhere earlier in the paper. What is wrong?", "choices": ["Nothing — conclusions are for new ideas", "A conclusion summarizes and draws out what the paper showed; new claims with no evidence behind them do not belong there", "Conclusions should be longer than the results section", "The proposal should be in the abstract instead"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "Correct: (b). The conclusion can only draw on evidence the paper already presented; a new proposal needs its own analysis. (a) is wrong: conclusions are for summing up, not for launching new arguments. (c) is wrong: length is not the issue. (d) is wrong: the abstract summarizes the paper too — it cannot carry a claim the paper never makes."},
      {"module": 10, "q": "Your slide shows the full regression table — 12 numbers in 10-point type. What is wrong?", "choices": ["Nothing — more numbers means more proof", "The back row cannot read it, and the audience cannot absorb 12 numbers at once; one slide, one idea, one number", "The table should be in 8-point type", "Regression tables never belong in talks"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "Correct: (b). A talk is not the paper: the audience reads one idea per slide, in type big enough for the back row. (a) is wrong: unreadable numbers prove nothing. (c) is wrong: smaller type makes it worse. (d) is wrong: one key number from the table belongs on a slide — all twelve do not."},
      {"module": 10, "q": "An audience member asks about a variable you did not include in the regression. What is the best response?", "choices": ["'That variable does not matter'", "Name it as a stated limit of the study and the follow-up you would run next", "Change the subject", "Repeat the abstract"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "Correct: (b). Short, honest, and pointed at the paper's own limits — the audience respects a speaker who knows what the study cannot do. (a) is wrong: you cannot dismiss a variable you never tested. (c) is wrong: evasion is visible. (d) is wrong: the abstract does not answer new questions."},
      {"module": 10, "q": "You have 15 minutes and 12 slides planned, each with one idea. A colleague says to add 8 more slides of robustness checks. Why decline?", "choices": ["Robustness checks are unimportant", "20 slides in 15 minutes is 45 seconds each — the audience will read nothing, and the checks belong in the paper", "The audience dislikes robustness", "Slides must never exceed 12"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "Correct: (b). At 45 seconds a slide, nothing is absorbed; the paper carries the checks and the talk can mention them in one sentence. (a) is wrong: robustness checks matter — they just do not belong on slides. (c) is wrong: the audience is not the problem, the time is. (d) is wrong: there is no fixed cap — the cap is what the minutes allow."},
      {"module": 10, "q": "In rehearsal you finish the talk in 9 minutes instead of 15. What does that tell you?", "choices": ["The talk is perfect — shorter is always better", "You are rushing or the slides are too thin: slow down, explain the key numbers, and check the talk actually fills the time", "Rehearsal does not matter", "Add 6 more slides immediately"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "Correct: (b). Finishing at 60% of the time means the audience is being rushed past the argument, or the argument is thinner than the slides suggest. (a) is wrong: a talk that ends 6 minutes early has not done its job. (c) is wrong: rehearsal is what catches this before the audience does. (d) is wrong: more slides is not the fix for rushing — slowing down and explaining is."},
      {"module": 10, "q": "What should the first 60 seconds of the talk do?", "choices": ["Read the title page aloud", "State the question and the headline finding, so the audience knows why to listen", "Show every number in the paper", "Apologize for the small sample"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "Correct: (b). The opening earns the audience's attention: what you asked and what you found. (a) is wrong: the audience reads faster than you speak. (c) is wrong: 15 minutes cannot hold every number. (d) is wrong: the sample size belongs in the limits, not the opening."},
      {"module": 11, "q": "Demand is Qd = 80 − P and supply is Qs = 20 + P, so equilibrium is P = $30, Q = 50. Demand then rises to Qd = 100 − P. What is the new equilibrium?", "choices": ["P = $30, Q = 50", "P = $40, Q = 60", "P = $35, Q = 65", "P = $50, Q = 50"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "Correct: (b). 100 − P = 20 + P gives 80 = 2P, so P = $40 and Q = 100 − 40 = 60. (a) is wrong: that is the old equilibrium before demand rose. (c) is wrong: at P = $35, quantity demanded is 65 but quantity supplied is 55. (d) is wrong: at P = $50, quantity demanded is 50 but quantity supplied is 70."},
      {"module": 11, "q": "A shop raises its price from $10 to $12 and quantity sold falls from 100 to 80 a week. Using the midpoint method, the price elasticity of demand is about:", "choices": ["−0.50", "−1.22", "−2.00", "+1.22"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "Correct: (b). Percentage change in quantity is (80 − 100) ÷ 90 = −22.2%; in price it is (12 − 10) ÷ 11 = 18.2%; −22.2 ÷ 18.2 = −1.22. (a) is wrong: −0.50 would need a much smaller quantity response. (c) is wrong: −2.00 comes from dividing the 20% fall by the 10% rise without midpoints — the simple method, not the midpoint method. (d) is wrong: the sign is negative — demand slopes down."},
      {"module": 11, "q": "A shop sells 100 units a week at $20 (revenue $2,000). Demand is inelastic, with elasticity −0.4. The shop raises its price 10%. What happens to revenue?", "choices": ["It falls, because quantity falls", "It rises to about $2,112 — the 10% price rise outweighs the 4% quantity fall", "It stays at $2,000", "It falls to $1,800"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "Correct: (b). Quantity falls by 0.4 × 10% = 4%, to 96 units; the new price is $22, so revenue is 22 × 96 = $2,112. (a) is wrong: under inelastic demand the price effect beats the quantity effect. (c) is wrong: revenue is unchanged only at unit elasticity. (d) is wrong: $1,800 would be a 10% quantity fall, which is the elastic case."},
      {"module": 11, "q": "A monopolist faces demand P = 60 − Q and constant marginal cost of $10. Competition would produce 50 units at $10; the monopolist produces 25 at $35. What is the deadweight loss?", "choices": ["$125", "$250", "$312.50", "$625"], "answer": 2, "explanation": "Correct: (c). Deadweight loss is the triangle: ½ × (35 − 10) × (50 − 25) = ½ × 25 × 25 = $312.50. (a) is wrong: $125 is half of the price gap times only 10 units. (b) is wrong: $250 is the price gap times 10 units, not the triangle. (d) is wrong: $625 is the full rectangle, not half of it."},
      {"module": 11, "q": "Flu shots create $15 of external benefit per shot — fewer sick coworkers and customers. What is the efficient policy, and why?", "choices": ["A $15 tax on shots, to punish the externality", "A $15 subsidy per shot, so the person getting the shot faces the full social benefit", "A ban on flu shots", "Nothing — positive externalities fix themselves"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "Correct: (b). A subsidy equal to the marginal external benefit aligns the private decision with the social one. (a) is wrong: a tax is the fix for a negative externality, and it would shrink shots further. (c) is wrong: banning shots destroys the benefit. (d) is wrong: left alone, people ignore the benefit to others and too few shots are taken."},
      {"module": 12, "q": "Nominal GDP is $30,000 billion and the GDP deflator is 120 (base year = 100). What is real GDP?", "choices": ["$36,000 billion", "$30,000 billion", "$25,000 billion", "$250 billion"], "answer": 2, "explanation": "Correct: (c). Real GDP is nominal divided by the deflator over 100: 30,000 ÷ 1.20 = $25,000 billion. (a) is wrong: that multiplies by 1.20 instead of dividing. (b) is wrong: that is nominal GDP, unadjusted for prices. (d) is wrong: that divides by 120 instead of 1.20."},
      {"module": 12, "q": "A worker deposits $2,000 in a bank. The reserve requirement is 25%. At most, how much in total deposits can the banking system support from this deposit?", "choices": ["$2,000", "$8,000", "$500", "$50,000"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "Correct: (b). The money multiplier is 1 ÷ 0.25 = 4, so $2,000 can support 4 × $2,000 = $8,000 in total deposits. (a) is wrong: that is the deposit itself, before any lending. (c) is wrong: $500 is the required reserve on the deposit, not the total. (d) is wrong: that would need a 4% reserve requirement."},
      {"module": 12, "q": "$2,000 is invested today at 5% interest, compounded annually, for 8 years. About how much is it worth at the end?", "choices": ["$2,800", "$2,954.91", "$2,400", "$4,000"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "Correct: (b). 2,000 × 1.05^8 = 2,000 × 1.4775 = $2,954.91. (a) is wrong: that is 5% simple interest for 8 years (2,000 + 800). (c) is wrong: that would be about 2.3% a year. (d) is wrong: doubling takes about 14 years at 5%."},
      {"module": 12, "q": "A labor force of 160 million people has 8 million unemployed. What is the unemployment rate?", "choices": ["8%", "12.8%", "5%", "20%"], "answer": 2, "explanation": "Correct: (c). 8 ÷ 160 = 0.05 = 5%. (a) is wrong: 8 is the number of millions unemployed, not the rate. (b) is wrong: that multiplies 8 by 1.6. (d) is wrong: that would need 32 million unemployed."},
      {"module": 12, "q": "In the teaching-sample regression (wage = −14.70 + 2.425 × schooling), a worker with 18 years of schooling earns $31 an hour. What is the residual?", "choices": ["+$2.05", "−$2.05", "$0", "+$28.95"], "answer": 0, "explanation": "Correct: (a). Predicted wage at 18 years is −14.70 + 2.425 × 18 = $28.95; the residual is actual minus predicted, 31 − 28.95 = +$2.05. (b) is wrong: that is the wrong sign — the worker earns above the prediction. (c) is wrong: the residual is zero only if actual equals predicted. (d) is wrong: $28.95 is the predicted wage, not the residual."}
    ]
  },
  "final": {
    "title": "Economics Core Comprehensive II",
    "minutes": 60,
    "passPercent": 70,
    "coverage": "Comprehensive review. Practice exam — original questions in the app's own course style. 70% is the app's own course standard, not a credential claim.",
    "questions": [
      {"module": 1, "q": "A student wants the capstone to 'prove that college pays.' What is wrong with that framing?", "choices": ["Nothing — that is the point of the capstone", "A 10-worker teaching sample can show an association, not prove a causal claim about the whole labor market", "The capstone is about proving things, not estimating them", "College does not pay"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "Correct: (b). The paper estimates an association on a teaching sample; proof of causation for a whole labor market is a claim no such study can make. (a) is wrong: the point is a careful, checkable estimate, not proof. (c) is wrong: the capstone estimates — it does not prove. (d) is wrong: the paper's own estimate points the other way."},
      {"module": 1, "q": "Which of these is the strongest evidence that a capstone paper is original?", "choices": ["It is longer than the textbook chapter", "It asks a new question, uses new data, or brings a new angle to an old question", "It cites more papers than anyone else", "It uses the same data as a published paper"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "Correct: (b). Originality is a new question, new data, or a new angle — something the student adds. (a) is wrong: length is not originality. (c) is wrong: citation counts measure reading, not contribution. (d) is wrong: the same data with the same question is a reproduction."},
      {"module": 1, "q": "Your abstract is 400 words long. What is the problem?", "choices": ["Nothing — abstracts should be as long as possible", "An abstract is a summary of about 150 words; at 400 words it is a second introduction", "Abstracts are never read", "The abstract should be 400 words minimum"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "Correct: (b). Four sentences — question, method, result, implication — in about 150 words; 400 words means it has stopped summarizing and started arguing. (a) is wrong: an abstract's job is brevity. (c) is wrong: the abstract is the most-read part of the paper. (d) is wrong: there is no 400-word minimum."},
      {"module": 2, "q": "A researcher writes H0: β = 0 and H1: β > 0 for the effect of tutoring hours on test scores. If the data reject H0, what has she shown?", "choices": ["That tutoring causes higher scores", "That the data are inconsistent with no effect — evidence of a positive association, not proof of causation", "That the slope is exactly the estimated value", "That tutoring has no effect"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "Correct: (b). Rejection says the no-effect claim does not fit the data; it does not rule out omitted variables or reverse causation. (a) is wrong: a test cannot prove causation. (c) is wrong: the estimate has a standard error around it. (d) is wrong: that is what failing to reject would suggest."},
      {"module": 2, "q": "Which of these is a testable research question?", "choices": ["'Is remote work better?'", "'Do remote workers in the 2025 survey report higher job satisfaction than office workers?'", "'Should everyone work remotely?'", "'Remote work is the future'"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "Correct: (b). It names a population, two groups, and a measurable outcome — a result could prove it wrong. (a) is wrong: 'better' is a value judgment with no measure. (c) is wrong: 'should' asks for a prescription, not a test. (d) is wrong: that is a slogan about the future."},
      {"module": 2, "q": "The 'how much' narrowing cut asks:", "choices": ["Who the population is", "What the variables are", "How big the effect is — the size of the payoff, not just its direction", "What the comparison group is"], "answer": 2, "explanation": "Correct: (c). 'How much' is the effect size — dollars per year of schooling, not just whether the effect is positive. (a) is wrong: 'who' names the population. (b) is wrong: 'what' names the variables. (d) is wrong: 'compared to what' names the comparison group."},
      {"module": 3, "q": "A new study finds a 9% return to a year of schooling. How does that compare with the literature surveyed by Card (1999)?", "choices": ["It contradicts the literature", "It sits inside the surveyed 5–10% range, so it is consistent with what is already known", "It is far above the range", "It is far below the range"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "Correct: (b). 9% falls between 5% and 10% — the study agrees with the survey rather than challenging it. (a) is wrong: agreement is not contradiction. (c) is wrong: far above would be 20% or 40%. (d) is wrong: far below would be 1% or 2%."},
      {"module": 3, "q": "Why does the literature review cite both Mincer (1974) and Card (1999)?", "choices": ["To pad the references", "Mincer supplies the model that makes the return estimable; Card supplies the surveyed range of estimates to compare against", "They agree with each other on everything", "Card proved Mincer wrong"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "Correct: (b). Each citation does a different job: Mincer gives the earnings function, Card gives the benchmark range. (a) is wrong: citations must do work. (c) is wrong: they do different things, 25 years apart. (d) is wrong: Card surveyed the literature Mincer started; he did not disprove it."},
      {"module": 3, "q": "A review reports only the largest return to schooling ever published and ignores the rest. What is wrong?", "choices": ["Nothing — the largest estimate is the best one", "It misleads the reader about what is known: a review must show the range and where studies disagree, not just the biggest number", "Large estimates are always wrong", "Reviews should never report numbers"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "Correct: (b). A review's job is the whole picture — the range, the agreement, the disagreement — and the largest estimate alone is a cherry-picked picture. (a) is wrong: the largest estimate is usually the most inflated one. (c) is wrong: large estimates are not always wrong, but they are not the whole story. (d) is wrong: numbers are exactly what a review should report."},
      {"module": 4, "q": "You survey the same 50 firms every quarter for 3 years. What kind of data is this?", "choices": ["Cross-sectional", "Time-series", "Panel — the same firms followed over time", "Experimental"], "answer": 2, "explanation": "Correct: (c). The same 50 firms observed over 12 quarters is panel data. (a) is wrong: cross-sectional data observes firms once. (b) is wrong: time series follows one firm over time. (d) is wrong: nothing was randomly assigned."},
      {"module": 4, "q": "A regression has Sxy = 120, Sxx = 60, mean x = 10, and mean y = 30. What is the intercept?", "choices": ["30", "10", "2", "20"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "Correct: (b). The slope is 120 ÷ 60 = 2, and the intercept is mean y minus slope times mean x: 30 − 2 × 10 = 10. (a) is wrong: 30 is the mean of y. (c) is wrong: 2 is the slope. (d) is wrong: 20 is the slope times mean x, only half the calculation."},
      {"module": 4, "q": "A researcher drops the intercept and forces the regression line through the origin. When is that defensible?", "choices": ["Always — intercepts are never useful", "Only when theory says the outcome is zero when the explanatory variable is zero", "When the sample is small", "When R² is low"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "Correct: (b). Forcing the line through zero is a claim about the world — it is right only when zero input truly means zero output. (a) is wrong: the intercept is part of the regression and usually belongs there. (c) is wrong: sample size has nothing to do with it. (d) is wrong: a low R² is a reason to rethink the model, not to drop the intercept."},
      {"module": 5, "q": "Five quiz scores are 60, 70, 80, 90, and 100. What are the mean, the median, and the sample standard deviation?", "choices": ["Mean 80, median 80, SD about 15.81", "Mean 80, median 90, SD 10", "Mean 75, median 80, SD about 15.81", "Mean 80, median 80, SD 25"], "answer": 0, "explanation": "Correct: (a). The mean is 400 ÷ 5 = 80; the median is the middle value, 80; the squared deviations sum to 1,000, so the sample variance is 1,000 ÷ 4 = 250 and the SD is √250 ≈ 15.81. (b) is wrong: the median is 80, not 90, and the SD is not 10. (c) is wrong: the mean is 80, not 75. (d) is wrong: 25 would be the variance divided by 10, not the SD."},
      {"module": 5, "q": "A sample has a mean of 50, a median of 45, and a standard deviation of 10. What does mean above median suggest?", "choices": ["Left skew — a few very low values drag the mean down", "Right skew — a few high values pull the mean above the median", "A symmetric distribution", "That the median is computed wrong"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "Correct: (b). When the mean sits above the median, high values are pulling the average up — the right tail is longer. (a) is wrong: that pattern gives mean below median. (c) is wrong: a symmetric distribution has mean and median about equal. (d) is wrong: the gap is a property of the data, not an arithmetic error."},
      {"module": 5, "q": "Which is more sensitive to a single extreme outlier, the mean or the median — and why?", "choices": ["The median — it moves to the outlier", "The mean — it adds the outlier's full value into the total, while the median only cares about the middle order", "Both equally", "Neither is affected by outliers"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "Correct: (b). The mean sums every value, so one huge value drags it; the median only needs the middle position, which barely moves. (a) is wrong: the median resists outliers — that is its strength. (c) is wrong: they react very differently. (d) is wrong: the mean is plainly affected."},
      {"module": 6, "q": "A regression has R² = 0.81. What share of the variation in the outcome is left unexplained?", "choices": ["81%", "19%", "0.81%", "Cannot tell from R²"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "Correct: (b). R² is the explained share, so 1 − 0.81 = 0.19, or 19%, is unexplained. (a) is wrong: 81% is the explained share. (c) is wrong: that confuses the decimal with a percentage. (d) is wrong: the unexplained share is exactly what R² implies."},
      {"module": 6, "q": "In a simple regression, the correlation between the two variables is −0.6. What is R²?", "choices": ["−0.6", "0.6", "0.36", "−0.36"], "answer": 2, "explanation": "Correct: (c). R² is the squared correlation: (−0.6)² = 0.36 — the negative sign disappears when squared. (a) is wrong: that is the correlation itself. (b) is wrong: that is the absolute correlation, not its square. (d) is wrong: R² can never be negative."},
      {"module": 6, "q": "A regression of sales on advertising finds a slope of 3.2 with a standard error of 0.8. The t-statistic is 4.0 and the 5% critical value is 2.0. What do you conclude?", "choices": ["Fail to reject the null", "Reject the null — the slope is statistically significant", "Advertising causes sales", "The sample is too small"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "Correct: (b). 4.0 > 2.0, so the null of a zero slope is rejected. (a) is wrong: that is what a small t would give. (c) is wrong: significance is not causation. (d) is wrong: the t-statistic already accounts for the sample size through the standard error."},
      {"module": 6, "q": "For the same regression (slope 3.2, standard error 0.8, critical value 2.0), the 95% confidence interval for the slope is:", "choices": ["1.6 to 4.8", "2.4 to 4.0", "0.0 to 3.2", "3.2 to 4.8"], "answer": 0, "explanation": "Correct: (a). 3.2 ± 2.0 × 0.8 = 3.2 ± 1.6 = 1.6 to 4.8. (b) is wrong: that uses a margin of 0.8, one standard error instead of two. (c) is wrong: the interval does not include zero. (d) is wrong: that starts at the slope instead of centering on it."},
      {"module": 7, "q": "A drug trial with 10,000 patients finds the new drug improves recovery by 0.1 percentage points, with p < 0.01. What is the right verdict?", "choices": ["A breakthrough — the p-value is tiny", "Statistically significant but economically trivial: 0.1 points is too small to matter to patients", "Not significant — the sample is too big", "Proof the drug works well"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "Correct: (b). With 10,000 patients even a negligible effect clears the significance bar; 0.1 points is a trivial gain for a patient. (a) is wrong: the p-value measures precision, not size. (c) is wrong: a big sample makes significance easier, not harder. (d) is wrong: 'works well' is about size, and the size is 0.1 points."},
      {"module": 7, "q": "A gym finds the members who attend most often lose the most weight. What is the most plausible rival story?", "choices": ["Attendance causes weight loss, with no other explanation", "The most motivated members both attend often and watch their diet, so motivation drives both", "Weight loss causes attendance", "The gym's scale is broken"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "Correct: (b). Motivation is an omitted variable that plausibly raises both attendance and weight loss. (a) is wrong: it rules out every alternative by assertion. (c) is wrong: losing weight does not make someone attend more. (d) is wrong: a broken scale is a measurement problem, not a rival explanation."},
      {"module": 7, "q": "A regression of crime on police spending omits poverty. Poverty raises crime, and poorer cities spend more on police. How is the spending coefficient biased?", "choices": ["Downward — it overstates the crime-reducing effect of spending", "Upward — the omitted poverty effect pushes it up, hiding the crime-reducing effect of spending", "Not biased — poverty is not in the data", "Upward, because police cause poverty"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "Correct: (b). Bias equals the omitted effect (poverty raises crime: positive) times the link between the omitted and included variables (poverty is positively related to spending), which is positive — so the spending coefficient is pushed up, making police look less effective than they are. (a) is wrong: the bias is upward, not downward. (c) is wrong: leaving a variable out is exactly what creates the bias. (d) is wrong: the causation runs from poverty to spending, not the other way."},
      {"module": 7, "q": "A 95% confidence interval for a policy's effect is −0.5 to 2.5. What can you conclude?", "choices": ["The effect is zero", "The effect is large", "The interval includes zero, so the effect is not statistically significant — and it is wide, so the study is too imprecise to rule out a large effect either", "The policy definitely works"], "answer": 2, "explanation": "Correct: (c). Including zero means no significant effect, but the interval also reaches 2.5, so a large effect is still possible — the study is imprecise, not conclusive. (a) is wrong: including zero is not proof the effect is zero. (b) is wrong: the interval also includes negative values. (d) is wrong: the interval includes zero and negative effects."},
      {"module": 8, "q": "A program pays $2,000 a year for 20 years. At a 6% discount rate its present value is about $22,940. If the discount rate were lower, the present value would be:", "choices": ["Lower — future money is worth less when rates fall", "Higher — future money is discounted less heavily", "Unchanged", "Negative"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "Correct: (b). A lower rate discounts each future payment less, so the same $2,000-a-year stream is worth more today. (a) is wrong: that has the direction backwards. (c) is wrong: the present value depends directly on the rate. (d) is wrong: the payments are positive, so the present value stays positive."},
      {"module": 8, "q": "A program costs $12,000 now and returns $1,000 every year forever. At a 5% discount rate, what is its net present value?", "choices": ["−$12,000", "$0", "+$8,000", "+$20,000"], "answer": 2, "explanation": "Correct: (c). A perpetuity of $1,000 a year at 5% is worth 1,000 ÷ 0.05 = $20,000 today; net of the $12,000 cost that is +$8,000. (a) is wrong: that ignores the benefits entirely. (b) is wrong: the benefits exceed the cost. (d) is wrong: $20,000 is the present value of the benefits, before subtracting the cost."},
      {"module": 8, "q": "Why discount future benefits at all, rather than just adding them up?", "choices": ["Because future dollars are worth less than today's — a dollar today can be invested, and people prefer sooner to later", "Because discounting makes benefits look bigger", "Because the arithmetic requires it", "Future benefits do not matter at all"], "answer": 0, "explanation": "Correct: (a). A dollar today can earn interest and be spent now, so a dollar in 30 years is worth less today — discounting prices that fact in. (b) is wrong: discounting shrinks benefits, it does not enlarge them. (c) is wrong: the arithmetic serves the economics, not the other way round. (d) is wrong: future benefits matter — they are just worth less than the same dollars today."},
      {"module": 9, "q": "A regression table shows the slope as 2.43, with no units anywhere. What is missing?", "choices": ["Nothing — numbers speak for themselves", "The units: $2.43 per hour per extra year of schooling, so the reader knows what the number means", "The p-value only", "The sample size only"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "Correct: (b). 2.43 of what, per what? Without units the reader cannot tell dollars per hour from percent or points. (a) is wrong: a bare number is meaningless. (c) is wrong: a p-value does not supply units. (d) is wrong: the sample size does not supply units either."},
      {"module": 9, "q": "You learned Card's 5–10% range from a lecture slide, not from reading Card (1999). How do you cite it honestly?", "choices": ["Cite Card (1999) as if you read it", "Cite the lecture or the secondary source where you actually found it — never claim to have read work you have not", "Do not cite it at all", "Cite it as your own estimate"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "Correct: (b). A citation is a claim that you read the work; cite what you actually read, or say honestly where you found it. (a) is wrong: citing unread work is dishonest. (c) is wrong: the range still needs a source — the lecture is one. (d) is wrong: the range is Card's, not yours."},
      {"module": 9, "q": "The end-to-end read finds the abstract says n = 10 but a table says n = 12. What is the fix?", "choices": ["Leave it — readers will not notice", "Find which one is wrong, correct it, and check every other place the sample size appears", "Change the abstract to 12 without checking", "Delete the table"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "Correct: (b). One of the two numbers is wrong, and the sample size appears in several places — the read must find the error and chase it everywhere. (a) is wrong: readers notice contradictions first. (c) is wrong: changing a number without checking may enshrine the wrong one. (d) is wrong: deleting evidence hides the problem."},
      {"module": 10, "q": "Your talk's headline number is the R² of 0.96. Why is that the wrong headline?", "choices": ["R² is the most important number", "R² measures fit, not the finding — the headline should be the slope, the $2.43 payoff per year of schooling", "The audience loves R²", "0.96 is too small to mention"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "Correct: (b). The finding is what schooling pays — $2.43 an hour per year — while R² only says the line fits well. (a) is wrong: fit is not the finding. (c) is wrong: the audience cares about the result, not the fit statistic. (d) is wrong: 0.96 is a very high R² — and still not the headline."},
      {"module": 10, "q": "An audience member says, 'Your sample is only 10 people.' What is the best reply?", "choices": ["'You are wrong — 10 is plenty'", "'Yes — it is a teaching sample, and that is the point: every number is checkable by hand, while the literature's large samples support the direction of the finding'", "'The R² is 0.96, so the sample does not matter'", "'I refuse to answer'"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "Correct: (b). Short, honest, and pointed back to the paper's own argument: transparency is the contribution, and the literature supplies the large-sample evidence. (a) is wrong: 10 is not plenty, and denying it destroys credibility. (c) is wrong: fit does not answer a sample-size question. (d) is wrong: refusing to answer is the worst reply of all."},
      {"module": 10, "q": "Why end the talk by naming the paper's limits instead of hiding them?", "choices": ["To make the paper look weak", "Because an audience that hears the limits from you trusts the rest of the talk; limits found by the audience first destroy credibility", "Limits are only for the paper, never the talk", "Because the tutor requires it"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "Correct: (b). Naming limits is a credibility move — the audience trusts a speaker who is honest about what the study cannot do. (a) is wrong: honest limits make the paper look stronger, not weaker. (c) is wrong: limits belong in the talk exactly because the audience will ask about them. (d) is wrong: the reason is the audience's trust, not a rule."},
      {"module": 11, "q": "Demand is Qd = 200 − 4P and supply is Qs = 40 + 4P, so equilibrium is P = $20, Q = 120. A $5 per-unit tax is placed on buyers. What is the new quantity, and what do buyers pay?", "choices": ["Q = 120, buyers pay $20", "Q = 110, buyers pay $22.50", "Q = 100, buyers pay $25", "Q = 110, buyers pay $17.50"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "Correct: (b). The tax shifts demand down by $5: 200 − 4(P + 5) = 180 − 4P; setting that equal to supply gives P = $17.50 received by sellers, so buyers pay $22.50 and quantity is 180 − 70 = 110. (a) is wrong: that is the pre-tax equilibrium. (c) is wrong: quantity falls by 10, not 20. (d) is wrong: $17.50 is what sellers receive, not what buyers pay."},
      {"module": 11, "q": "A price falls 8% and quantity demanded rises 4%. The price elasticity of demand is:", "choices": ["−0.5 (inelastic)", "−2.0 (elastic)", "+0.5", "−4"], "answer": 0, "explanation": "Correct: (a). +4% ÷ −8% = −0.5, and |−0.5| < 1, so demand is inelastic. (b) is wrong: −2.0 would need quantity to rise 16%. (c) is wrong: the sign is negative — demand slopes down. (d) is wrong: −4 is the percentage quantity change with the wrong sign handling, not the elasticity."},
      {"module": 11, "q": "A monopolist faces demand P = 100 − 2Q and constant marginal cost of $20. Competition would produce 40 units at $20. What is the deadweight loss from monopoly?", "choices": ["$200", "$400", "$800", "$0"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "Correct: (b). Marginal revenue is 100 − 4Q; setting MR = MC gives Q = 20 and P = $60. Deadweight loss is ½ × (60 − 20) × (40 − 20) = $400. (a) is wrong: $200 is half the triangle — it uses a quantity gap of 10 instead of 20. (c) is wrong: $800 is the full rectangle, not half of it. (d) is wrong: monopoly restricts output, so deadweight loss is positive."},
      {"module": 11, "q": "Demand is Qd = 120 − 2P and supply is Qs = 20 + 2P, so equilibrium is P = $25, Q = 70. A price floor of $30 is imposed. What happens?", "choices": ["A shortage of 20 units", "A surplus of 20 units — quantity demanded is 60 but quantity supplied is 80", "The market clears at 70 units", "Quantity demanded rises to 80"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "Correct: (b). At $30, quantity demanded is 120 − 60 = 60 and quantity supplied is 20 + 60 = 80 — a surplus of 20. (a) is wrong: a surplus, not a shortage, comes from a floor above equilibrium. (c) is wrong: the market does not clear above equilibrium. (d) is wrong: 80 is the quantity supplied, not demanded."},
      {"module": 12, "q": "Nominal GDP grows 6% in a year while prices rise 4%. About how much did real GDP grow?", "choices": ["10%", "About 2%", "6%", "4%"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "Correct: (b). Real growth is roughly nominal growth minus inflation: 6% − 4% ≈ 2% (exactly 1.06 ÷ 1.04 − 1 = 1.9%). (a) is wrong: that adds the two rates instead of subtracting. (c) is wrong: 6% is nominal growth, before removing inflation. (d) is wrong: 4% is the inflation rate, not real growth."},
      {"module": 12, "q": "A basket of goods costs $150 today and cost $125 in the base year. What is the CPI today (base year = 100)?", "choices": ["125", "120", "150", "20"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "Correct: (b). CPI is the basket cost today divided by the base-year cost, times 100: 150 ÷ 125 × 100 = 120. (a) is wrong: that is the base-year cost. (c) is wrong: that is today's cost in dollars, not the index. (d) is wrong: 20 is the percentage rise from the base year, and the index is 100 + 20 = 120."},
      {"module": 12, "q": "With a marginal propensity to consume of 0.75, the spending multiplier is 4. A $60 billion increase in government purchases raises GDP by about:", "choices": ["$60 billion", "$80 billion", "$240 billion", "$15 billion"], "answer": 2, "explanation": "Correct: (c). $60 billion × 4 = $240 billion — the initial spending is re-spent again and again. (a) is wrong: that ignores the multiplier entirely. (b) is wrong: that would be a multiplier of 1.33. (d) is wrong: that divides instead of multiplying."},
      {"module": 12, "q": "$5,000 received in 7 years, at a 6% discount rate, is worth today about:", "choices": ["$7,518", "$3,325.29", "$5,000", "$2,500"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "Correct: (b). 5,000 ÷ 1.06^7 = 5,000 ÷ 1.5036 = $3,325.29. (a) is wrong: that is the future value of $5,000 in 7 years at 6%. (c) is wrong: future money is worth less than present money. (d) is wrong: that would be a 50% discount over 7 years."}
    ]
  }
};
